        {
          "text": " — against two roots, "
        },
        {
          "text": "4·7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·10",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", both on the inside strings, exactly as chapter 3 found. Neither number alone is unusual: the C and A forms also hold two roots, and the C, A and G forms also hold three "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "s. It's the combination that makes this window what it is — three of the chapter's darkest note against only two places to land, and a hand that runs it without planning where it stops tends to sound restless rather than settled."
        }
      ]
    },
